## 4. Error Handling Pattern

Every System API must implement the global error handler pattern:

```
global-error-handler.xml          ← dedicated file, referenced via defaultErrorHandler-ref
  ├── on-error-continue            ← in global handler (returns structured response)
  └── on-error-propagate           ← in sub-flows (bubbles up to global handler)

errors/customErrors.dwl            ← error type → HTTP status code mappings
errors/errorCatalog.dwl            ← error codes, reasons, messages
```

**Standard error → HTTP status mappings:**

| Mule Error Type | HTTP Status |
|---|---|
| `HTTP:BAD_REQUEST` | 400 |
| `HTTP:UNAUTHORIZED` | 401 |
| `HTTP:NOT_FOUND` | 404 |
| `APP:CONNECTIVITY_FAILED` | 503 |
| `HTTP:INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR` | 500 |
| `MULE:UNKNOWN` | derived from error details |

Add **502** (Bad Gateway) when the API calls an external system that returns its own errors (e.g., Salesforce Composite API failure). This is distinct from 503 (the API itself is unavailable).

**External HTTP retries:** Use `until-successful` for connectivity retries on external HTTP calls where appropriate. Connectivity failures should raise `APP:CONNECTIVITY_FAILED`.

**Error handler logging — two loggers required:**
1. `build-error-log-without-payload` (INFO, `com.insulet.general`) — error context with `errorType`, `errorDescription`, `cause`, `errorCode`; no payload; **`tracepoint="EXCEPTION"` must be set explicitly** (connector default `FLOW_START` is wrong in error context)
2. `build-error-log-with-masked-payload` OR `build-error-log-with-encrypted-payload` (DEBUG) — protected payload for triage; also requires explicit `tracepoint="EXCEPTION"`

**Async/event-driven APIs additionally require:**
- `on-error-continue` to prevent message loss
- Dead Letter Queue (DLQ) publish on failure
- Notification on DLQ publish failure

**Canonical rule:** `agents/review/rules/review-error-handling.mdc`

