## 10. RAML / API Spec Standards

- **Business endpoints MUST use `CommonLib.generic` resourceType** — this is mandatory, not optional
- Standard health checks: always include `/ping` (liveliness) and `/health-check` (readiness) using `CommonLib.liveliness-probe` and `CommonLib.readiness-probe` resource types
- **`baseUri` is MANDATORY**: `baseUri: https://api.example.com/{project-name}/api/{version}`
- Library: `CommonLib` pointing to `exchange_modules/e0bc8c96-9dbd-48bc-915e-3e29577af6b3/common-fragment-library/1.0.2/common-fragment-library.raml`
- Folder names: **camelCase** (`dataTypes`, `enumTypes`, `traits`, `examples`)
- Non-root files: **TitleCase** (`CreateCustomerProfileRequest.raml`, `ErrorResponsesTrait.raml`)
- Example files in `examples/`: TitleCase (e.g., `CreateCustomerProfileRequestExample.raml`) — do not use lowercase-hyphenated
- `type` (resource type) is applied to **resources** only — not to methods

**Mandatory traits for all business endpoints:**
| Trait | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `CommonLib.common-error-handler` | Exclusive source of 4xx/5xx error responses — do NOT manually define error codes |
| `CommonLib.common-headers` | Common request/response headers |
| `CommonLib.system-headers` | System-level headers |
| `client-id-required` | API authentication |

**Prohibited — manual error definitions:** Do NOT define 4xx or 5xx responses in the root RAML for business endpoints. All error handling inherits from `CommonLib.common-error-handler` in the `is:` section.

**Response data wrapping:** Every response example and its `DataType` must have a top-level `data` element as the parent.

**Canonical rule:** `agents/raml/rules/gen-raml-implementation.mdc`

---

## 11. Field & Example Naming — camelCase Everywhere

All JSON field names in request payloads, response payloads, input tables, and mapping tables
**must be camelCase**. This applies to every example and every table in the generated document.

- ✅ `merchantCustomerId`, `customerProfileId`, `expirationDate`, `firstName`
- ❌ `merchant_customer_id`, `MerchantCustomerId`, `MERCHANTCUSTOMERID`

---

## 12. HTTP Status Codes by Method (fixed — do not deviate)

| Method | Success | Client Errors | Server Error |
|---|---|---|---|
| GET | 200 | 400, 401, 404 | 500 |
| POST (create) | 201 | 400, 401 | 500 |
| PUT / PATCH | 200 | 400, 401 | 500 |
| DELETE | 204 | 400, 401, 404 | 500 |

Add **502** (Bad Gateway) when the API calls an external system that can return its own errors (e.g., Salesforce Composite API, external REST API).
Add **503** when the API itself is unavailable due to a connectivity failure (`APP:CONNECTIVITY_FAILED`).
Add **409** for duplicate-resource conflicts (POST only).

---

## 13. Standard Error Response Format (fixed — always the same)

Every error response in Insulet APIs uses this format. Never deviate:

```json
{
  "correlationId": "a1b2c3d4-1234-5678-abcd-ef1234567890",
  "errorCode": "NAMESPACE:ERROR_CODE",
  "description": "Human-readable description of the failure.",
  "errorDetails": [
    {
      "message": "Downstream or validation error message.",
      "code": "NAMESPACE:ERROR_CODE"
    }
  ]
}
```

- `correlationId`: Mule-generated UUID for end-to-end tracing
- `errorCode`: `NAMESPACE:SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` (e.g., `AUTHNET:DUPLICATE_PROFILE`, `HTTP:BAD_REQUEST`, `SALESFORCE-COMPOSITE:VALIDATION`)
- `description`: Human-readable sentence — no internal implementation details (no flow names, DWL names, connector class names)
- `errorDetails`: one entry per downstream error; omit array if empty. Use same `NAMESPACE:SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` format for `code`. Use `message` for the human-readable downstream error message.

**Common error code namespaces:**
| Namespace | Used for |
|---|---|
| `HTTP:` | Standard HTTP errors (400, 401, 404, 500) |
| `SALESFORCE:` | Salesforce Connector errors (SOQL, connectivity) |
| `SALESFORCE-COMPOSITE:` | Salesforce Composite Connector errors |
| `APP:` | Custom application errors (`APP:CONNECTIVITY_FAILED`) |
| `MULE:` | Internal Mule runtime errors |
| `{SYSTEM}:` | External system errors (e.g., `AUTHNET:DUPLICATE_PROFILE`) |

