## 14. MUnit Testing Standards

These apply to the develop agent output and are documented here so the design can account for testability.

- **70% minimum code coverage** per test suite and overall
- **One test suite per Mule flow file** (one-to-one correlation)
- Each flow must have **at least one positive** (happy path) and **at least one negative** (error) test case
- **All external calls must be mocked** — no real HTTP, Salesforce, or MQ calls in unit tests
- Test suites under `src/test/munit/`; test resources under `src/test/resources/`
- Run via `mvn clean test -P<profile>` before committing

**Canonical rule:** `agents/review/rules/review-munit-testing.mdc`

---

## 15. Maven & POM Standards

- **Semantic versioning:** `major.minor.patch` (e.g., `1.0.17`). Development: `--SNAPSHOT` suffix
  - Major: breaking changes (renamed resources, structural model changes)
  - Minor: non-breaking additions (new endpoints, enhanced error handling)
  - Patch: bug fixes, refactoring
- **No hardcoded Exchange credentials in POM** — use `settings.xml` for Anypoint Exchange and Mule EE authentication
- **`settings.xml` required servers:** `anypoint-exchange-v3`, `eu-anypoint-exchange-v3`, `mulesoft-release-ee`
- RAML spec must be pulled from Anypoint Exchange as a dependency with `raml` classifier and `zip` type
- Keep RAML spec version in sync with the APIKit configuration

**Canonical rule:** `agents/review/rules/review-pom-maven-standards.mdc`

---

## 16. What Does NOT Belong in a Design Document

These items belong in implementation agents (`/use-raml`, `/use-develop`) — never in the design:
- RAML snippets or API spec code
- DataWeave (DWL) scripts, file names, or transformation logic
- Mule XML fragments or flow/sub-flow names used as headings
- MUnit test cases, test data, or coverage percentages
- Build or deployment scripts
- Internal connector operation names as primary text (e.g., `Execute Composite Graph`, `Query XML Stream`) — reference the connector name and its role, not the operation symbol
- Log variable names, DWL file names, or internal implementation symbols
